1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,160
On Thursday, NVIDIA lost more than $400 billion in a single morning, and it hadn't done anything

2
00:00:07,160 --> 00:00:08,160
wrong.

3
00:00:08,160 --> 00:00:09,160
Nobody had.

4
00:00:09,160 --> 00:00:10,160
No bank failed.

5
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:11,360
No law passed.

6
00:00:11,360 --> 00:00:12,800
The economy looked fine.

7
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:19,400
The S&P 500 still lost more than $2 trillion by mid-morning, and the NASDAQ needed just

8
00:00:19,400 --> 00:00:23,320
six minutes to give back a rally that was one day old.

9
00:00:23,320 --> 00:00:26,080
So why did the stock market just crash?

10
00:00:26,080 --> 00:00:28,320
And was this the AI bubble popping?

11
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:32,640
Best day after a midterm since 1982?

12
00:00:32,640 --> 00:00:34,040
Does this come with a receipt?

13
00:00:34,040 --> 00:00:36,080
The short answer is an election.

14
00:00:36,080 --> 00:00:42,600
600,000 chips stuck in Hong Kong and some very rich people locking in a very good year.

15
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:50,840
The long answer starts a month earlier, on the 6th of October, in Santa Clara, California.

16
00:00:50,840 --> 00:00:54,960
That day, NVIDIA closed at the highest price it had ever had.

17
00:00:54,960 --> 00:01:02,160
A month before, it was worth about $5.4 trillion, roughly $0.08 of every dollar in the whole

18
00:01:02,160 --> 00:01:03,720
S&P 500.

19
00:01:03,720 --> 00:01:10,040
If you own a plain index fund, the safe, boring kind everyone recommends, you own a lot of

20
00:01:10,040 --> 00:01:11,040
NVIDIA.

21
00:01:11,040 --> 00:01:17,360
Add the rest of the magnificent seven, Apple, Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta, and Tesla,

22
00:01:17,360 --> 00:01:22,520
and about $35 of every hundred in that fund sits on seven companies.

23
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:24,040
I'm diversified.

24
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:28,240
Seven of them are a third of the pie.

25
00:01:28,240 --> 00:01:31,240
That is a big slice.

26
00:01:31,240 --> 00:01:36,560
Even at the dot-com peak, the market wasn't this lopsided, and most of those giants are

27
00:01:36,560 --> 00:01:38,440
betting on the same thing.

28
00:01:38,440 --> 00:01:40,360
That bet is expensive.

29
00:01:40,360 --> 00:01:46,560
This year, the four biggest cloud companies plan to spend over $700 billion building data

30
00:01:46,560 --> 00:01:47,560
centers.

31
00:01:47,560 --> 00:01:49,560
Next year, more than a trillion.

32
00:01:49,560 --> 00:01:56,000
The biggest line on that shopping list says NVIDIA, whose last quarter brought in $96 billion,

33
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:00,120
more than double the year before.

34
00:02:00,120 --> 00:02:04,440
By late October, investors were staring at four cracks.

35
00:02:04,440 --> 00:02:05,440
Crack one.

36
00:02:05,440 --> 00:02:07,120
The money goes in a circle.

37
00:02:07,120 --> 00:02:13,040
NVIDIA invests in its own customers, the AI labs and chip rental firms that then buy NVIDIA

38
00:02:13,040 --> 00:02:14,040
chips.

39
00:02:14,040 --> 00:02:18,120
NVIDIA is a big mistake in CoreWeave, which rents those chips out and agreed to buy up

40
00:02:18,120 --> 00:02:23,480
to $6.3 billion of the computing power CoreWeave can't sell.

41
00:02:23,480 --> 00:02:26,400
NVIDIA's own finance chief knows how that sounds.

42
00:02:26,400 --> 00:02:29,360
We know some will call this circular financing.

43
00:02:29,360 --> 00:02:31,960
Here, here's a few billion dollars.

44
00:02:31,960 --> 00:02:34,880
Wow, what should I spend it on?

45
00:02:34,880 --> 00:02:36,440
Funny you should ask.

46
00:02:36,440 --> 00:02:37,440
Crack two.

47
00:02:37,440 --> 00:02:39,200
The chips are the collateral.

48
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:44,120
Customers like CoreWeave borrowed huge sums to buy chips, rented them out, and pledged

49
00:02:44,120 --> 00:02:46,280
those same chips to borrow more.

50
00:02:46,280 --> 00:02:51,200
At one point, CoreWeave owed over $5 for every dollar of equity.

51
00:02:51,200 --> 00:02:53,920
Fine, while a used chip holds its value.

52
00:02:53,920 --> 00:02:59,160
But NVIDIA brings out a new generation every year or two, and each one makes the last one

53
00:02:59,160 --> 00:03:00,400
worth less.

54
00:03:00,400 --> 00:03:04,920
And Michael Burry, who called the housing crash, says the giants book their servers as

55
00:03:04,920 --> 00:03:08,400
lasting longer than they do, which flatters profits.

56
00:03:08,400 --> 00:03:09,520
The nightmare.

57
00:03:09,520 --> 00:03:14,560
One borrower misses one payment, the lender grabs the chips to sell, and everyone learns

58
00:03:14,560 --> 00:03:18,280
that afternoon what a used AI chip is worth in a hurry.

59
00:03:18,280 --> 00:03:19,360
It hasn't happened.

60
00:03:19,360 --> 00:03:22,160
But every chip investor has thought about it.

61
00:03:22,160 --> 00:03:23,160
Crack three.

62
00:03:23,160 --> 00:03:24,160
Who's paying?

63
00:03:24,160 --> 00:03:30,240
By the end of July, NVIDIA was owed about $63 billion for chips it had shipped but not

64
00:03:30,240 --> 00:03:31,640
been paid for yet.

65
00:03:31,640 --> 00:03:36,480
And this year, just three buyers made up more than two-fifths of its sales.

66
00:03:36,480 --> 00:03:41,120
We're a restaurant where three tables order two of every five plates.

67
00:03:41,120 --> 00:03:43,280
And a lot of the food goes on a tab.

68
00:03:43,280 --> 00:03:44,280
Crack four.

69
00:03:44,280 --> 00:03:45,280
The plug.

70
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:46,280
You can buy a chip.

71
00:03:46,280 --> 00:03:48,840
You can't buy electricity that doesn't exist.

72
00:03:48,840 --> 00:03:53,720
In Northern Virginia, the data center capital of the world, the weight to hook a new building

73
00:03:53,720 --> 00:03:57,360
up to the grid had stretched to about seven years.

74
00:03:57,360 --> 00:04:00,640
And the power that does exist, somebody pays for.

75
00:04:00,640 --> 00:04:06,760
On America's biggest grid, across 13 states, power plants get paid just to stand ready

76
00:04:06,760 --> 00:04:09,360
and families cover that in their bills.

77
00:04:09,360 --> 00:04:11,720
Then the data centers piled in.

78
00:04:11,720 --> 00:04:17,280
Households there were paying an estimated $25 to $30 a month more than in 2024.

79
00:04:17,280 --> 00:04:22,040
And the grid's own watchdog blamed data centers first.

80
00:04:22,040 --> 00:04:24,120
Why is the electric bill up again?

81
00:04:24,120 --> 00:04:25,920
Hi, neighbor.

82
00:04:25,920 --> 00:04:28,320
Nobody reads a spending report.

83
00:04:28,320 --> 00:04:29,800
Everybody reads their power bill.

84
00:04:29,800 --> 00:04:30,800
Hold that thought.

85
00:04:30,800 --> 00:04:32,800
Wall Street had its own bad news.

86
00:04:32,800 --> 00:04:37,000
On October 29th, Google had its worst trading day ever.

87
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:40,120
The next day, Intel fell by a quarter.

88
00:04:40,120 --> 00:04:45,560
Chip stocks slid into a bear market, and Apple, the boring one, passed Nvidia in value.

89
00:04:45,560 --> 00:04:52,200
By the 2nd of November, the 47 biggest AI and chip stocks had lost about $5 trillion

90
00:04:52,200 --> 00:04:53,280
since the top.

91
00:04:53,280 --> 00:04:56,080
That's about what the whole dot-com crash wiped out.

92
00:04:56,080 --> 00:04:58,320
And that took two and a half years.

93
00:04:58,320 --> 00:05:00,280
This took four weeks.

94
00:05:00,280 --> 00:05:04,040
Then came the election.

95
00:05:04,040 --> 00:05:08,000
Tuesday, the 3rd of November, Democrats won the House.

96
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:09,400
Then they won the Senate.

97
00:05:09,400 --> 00:05:15,320
Further down the ballot, in 10 states, there were 41 local votes on data centers.

98
00:05:15,320 --> 00:05:17,240
Voters went against them in 40.

99
00:05:17,240 --> 00:05:19,400
21 of those were in Ohio.

100
00:05:19,400 --> 00:05:23,000
The one town that said yes was Harwood, North Dakota.

101
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:24,000
Ohio.

102
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:25,000
No.

103
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:26,000
Ohio.

104
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:27,000
No.

105
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:28,000
Ohio.

106
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:29,000
Ohio.

107
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:30,000
Hi.

108
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:31,000
Yes.

109
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:32,000
Over here.

110
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:34,040
That's the power bill, showing up at the ballot box.

111
00:05:34,040 --> 00:05:39,960
In Ohio, Sherrod Brown beat John Husted, who'd been called the face of data centers.

112
00:05:39,960 --> 00:05:47,680
In Texas, Elon Musk, the cycle's biggest donor, with at least $474 million, lost both big

113
00:05:47,680 --> 00:05:48,680
races.

114
00:05:48,680 --> 00:05:49,840
His reaction?

115
00:05:49,840 --> 00:05:53,440
Data center's in space, looking pretty appealing right now.

116
00:05:53,440 --> 00:05:55,960
Here's why a town vote moves Wall Street.

117
00:05:55,960 --> 00:06:01,040
A data center needs land, power, and a permit, and all three are local.

118
00:06:01,040 --> 00:06:06,800
Goldman Sachs figured moratoriums like these could hit about 45 percent of America's planned

119
00:06:06,800 --> 00:06:10,720
data center expansion, and the bigger fear was Washington.

120
00:06:10,720 --> 00:06:14,160
For years, AI was mostly left alone to build.

121
00:06:14,160 --> 00:06:19,760
Now, a Democratic Congress, elected the same night voters said no to data centers, might

122
00:06:19,760 --> 00:06:24,880
want rules on where data centers go, who pays for their power, and how fast the buildout

123
00:06:24,880 --> 00:06:25,880
runs.

124
00:06:26,800 --> 00:06:32,080
Robert Garcia had two words for AI executives, lawyer up.

125
00:06:32,080 --> 00:06:36,720
So data centers, so data centers.

126
00:06:36,720 --> 00:06:38,000
We were just leaving.

127
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,500
No rule has passed, not one.

128
00:06:40,500 --> 00:06:42,960
It was the fear of a rule that did the selling.

129
00:06:42,960 --> 00:06:47,760
That trillion dollar shopping list had picked up some small print, if they let us build

130
00:06:47,760 --> 00:06:50,560
it.

131
00:06:50,560 --> 00:06:53,520
And on Wednesday, Wall Street partied anyway.

132
00:06:53,520 --> 00:06:58,360
The Dow jumped 1,569 points, about 3 percent.

133
00:06:58,360 --> 00:07:04,480
It's best day after a midterm since 1982, and oil fell more than 9 percent.

134
00:07:04,480 --> 00:07:06,160
Traders loved the cheap oil.

135
00:07:06,160 --> 00:07:08,080
They hadn't gotten to the new Congress yet.

136
00:07:08,080 --> 00:07:10,160
The party lasted one day.

137
00:07:10,160 --> 00:07:13,960
That morning, Vice President J.D. Vance had posted on X.

138
00:07:13,960 --> 00:07:17,000
The reason we lost is the war.

139
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:18,560
The war with Iran.

140
00:07:18,560 --> 00:07:21,480
That raised hopes of a deal, and oil fell.

141
00:07:21,480 --> 00:07:25,600
By Thursday morning, traders doubted it, and oil climbed again.

142
00:07:25,600 --> 00:07:29,800
Then, before the opening bell, Reuters dropped the next problem.

143
00:07:29,800 --> 00:07:35,840
About 600,000 of Nvidia's H-200 chips were sitting in Hong Kong, waiting for Beijing

144
00:07:35,840 --> 00:07:37,000
to let them in.

145
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:42,640
Washington had cleared the sales last winter, case by case, with a 25 percent tariff.

146
00:07:42,640 --> 00:07:48,120
In August, Beijing let companies use them in Hong Kong, into mainland China, in bulk,

147
00:07:48,120 --> 00:07:49,120
still waiting.

148
00:07:49,120 --> 00:07:51,440
You said we can sell them.

149
00:07:51,440 --> 00:07:52,440
You can.

150
00:07:52,440 --> 00:07:54,120
25 percent, please.

151
00:07:54,120 --> 00:07:55,200
They're in Hong Kong.

152
00:07:55,200 --> 00:07:56,840
Can they come in?

153
00:07:56,840 --> 00:07:58,000
Not moving.

154
00:07:58,000 --> 00:07:59,000
Not yet.

155
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:05,400
The strange part, Nvidia had already taken a $400 million hit on H-200s that nobody

156
00:08:05,400 --> 00:08:11,360
bought, and the ones that did have buyers couldn't get through the door.

157
00:08:11,360 --> 00:08:12,360
9.30.

158
00:08:12,360 --> 00:08:14,160
The bell rings in New York.

159
00:08:14,160 --> 00:08:18,120
It takes the Nasdaq six minutes to give back the whole rally.

160
00:08:18,120 --> 00:08:19,600
So much for Wednesday.

161
00:08:19,600 --> 00:08:20,600
10.35.

162
00:08:20,600 --> 00:08:25,160
The Nasdaq is down 4 percent, and the Dow 700 points.

163
00:08:25,160 --> 00:08:31,160
Across the S&P 500, more than $2 trillion has vanished, and more than $400 billion of

164
00:08:31,160 --> 00:08:33,080
it is Nvidia alone.

165
00:08:33,080 --> 00:08:37,440
That's about twice Amazon's entire building budget for the year.

166
00:08:37,440 --> 00:08:38,440
11.17.

167
00:08:38,440 --> 00:08:43,080
The VIX, Wall Street's fear gauge, is up 65 percent.

168
00:08:43,080 --> 00:08:47,600
Think of it as the price of crash insurance, and everybody wants some.

169
00:08:47,600 --> 00:08:48,600
11.37.

170
00:08:48,600 --> 00:08:55,760
The Dow is on track for its worst second day after a midterm in records going back to 18.96.

171
00:08:55,760 --> 00:08:56,760
11.38.

172
00:08:56,760 --> 00:09:02,160
AMD's boss, Lisa Su, speaks up, with her stock down 15 percent.

173
00:09:02,160 --> 00:09:06,480
I think the market can sometimes be a little bit short-sighted.

174
00:09:06,480 --> 00:09:07,480
Ah!

175
00:09:07,480 --> 00:09:08,480
Ah!

176
00:09:08,480 --> 00:09:09,480
2.58.

177
00:09:09,480 --> 00:09:11,800
And this is where ordinary people come in.

178
00:09:11,800 --> 00:09:14,760
Maybe you're at work checking your phone under the desk.

179
00:09:14,760 --> 00:09:17,640
You own a plain S&P 500 fund.

180
00:09:17,640 --> 00:09:24,040
If it holds a quarter of a million dollars, Nvidia alone took about $990 out of it that

181
00:09:24,040 --> 00:09:25,040
morning.

182
00:09:25,040 --> 00:09:32,480
By now, small investors have sold, on net, $358 million of stock, the most since March

183
00:09:32,480 --> 00:09:33,480
2020.

184
00:09:33,480 --> 00:09:34,600
Buy the dip.

185
00:09:34,600 --> 00:09:36,240
Or sell the dip.

186
00:09:36,240 --> 00:09:37,560
Or sell the dip.

187
00:09:37,560 --> 00:09:43,840
Viraj Patel at Vanda Research called it, a total capitulation by retail investors, with

188
00:09:43,960 --> 00:09:46,080
no signs of buying the dip.

189
00:09:46,080 --> 00:09:47,080
306.

190
00:09:47,080 --> 00:09:52,080
Charlie McElegate, a strategist at Nomura, explains who's really selling.

191
00:09:52,080 --> 00:09:53,720
They're shooting the generals.

192
00:09:53,720 --> 00:09:56,560
The generals are the year's biggest winners.

193
00:09:56,560 --> 00:10:01,720
Hedge funds had made a fortune in 2026, and with two months to go, they sold the winners

194
00:10:01,720 --> 00:10:03,200
to lock it in.

195
00:10:03,200 --> 00:10:07,920
Computer-driven funds and funds on borrowed money were forced to sell, too, and nobody

196
00:10:07,920 --> 00:10:09,640
stepped in to buy.

197
00:10:09,640 --> 00:10:10,640
Great year.

198
00:10:10,640 --> 00:10:11,640
Let's bank it.

199
00:10:11,640 --> 00:10:12,640
Sell the winners.

200
00:10:12,640 --> 00:10:13,640
Which ones?

201
00:10:13,760 --> 00:10:14,760
Yes.

202
00:10:14,760 --> 00:10:15,760
Yes.

203
00:10:15,760 --> 00:10:19,280
Single-name stock moves are absolutely bonkers.

204
00:10:19,280 --> 00:10:20,280
Four o'clock.

205
00:10:20,280 --> 00:10:21,360
The closing bell.

206
00:10:21,360 --> 00:10:24,800
The Dow lost 1,833 points.

207
00:10:24,800 --> 00:10:27,320
The S&P fell over 4%.

208
00:10:27,320 --> 00:10:29,480
The Nasdaq fell 5.5%.

209
00:10:29,480 --> 00:10:35,920
Its biggest drop in 19 months, and is now in a correction, at least 10% below its high.

210
00:10:35,920 --> 00:10:39,320
Chip stocks had their worst day since March 2020.

211
00:10:39,320 --> 00:10:44,120
Nvidia closed at $181, down more than 9%.

212
00:10:44,120 --> 00:10:48,480
And while everything burned, Apploven rose almost 30%.

213
00:10:48,480 --> 00:10:54,040
And Goldman Sachs picked that day to name 109 new partners, its biggest class since

214
00:10:54,040 --> 00:10:55,040
2010.

215
00:10:55,040 --> 00:11:01,760
Honestly, honestly, honestly, great day to get promoted.

216
00:11:01,760 --> 00:11:05,320
It's easy to think a crash like this only hurts rich people.

217
00:11:05,320 --> 00:11:06,320
It doesn't.

218
00:11:06,320 --> 00:11:11,920
Around 70 million Americans save in a 401k, mostly through the same index funds.

219
00:11:11,920 --> 00:11:15,080
And the pensions of teachers and firefighters hold them, too.

220
00:11:15,080 --> 00:11:17,800
When the seven giants fall, they fall, too.

221
00:11:17,800 --> 00:11:19,880
It goes deeper than stocks.

222
00:11:19,880 --> 00:11:24,960
Data centers run on borrowed money, much of it raised from insurers and pension funds.

223
00:11:24,960 --> 00:11:29,360
PIMCO bought a big piece of the bonds for Metta's giant Louisiana data center with

224
00:11:29,360 --> 00:11:30,680
Saver's money.

225
00:11:30,680 --> 00:11:34,680
And Washington has moved to open 401ks to this kind of lending.

226
00:11:34,680 --> 00:11:39,240
So your retirement may be quietly lending to a half-built building.

227
00:11:39,240 --> 00:11:41,560
And some get hit without owning a share.

228
00:11:41,560 --> 00:11:42,880
The electricians.

229
00:11:42,880 --> 00:11:47,760
Some making $260,000 a year on data center jobs.

230
00:11:47,760 --> 00:11:49,360
And the diners that feed them.

231
00:11:49,360 --> 00:11:51,360
Thursday didn't stop that work.

232
00:11:51,360 --> 00:11:54,600
But if the spending stops, it lands there first.

233
00:11:54,600 --> 00:11:57,580
If you're 25, Thursday was a sale.

234
00:11:57,580 --> 00:12:04,600
If you're 62 and retiring in the spring, it was a very long day.

235
00:12:04,600 --> 00:12:06,760
So who do you get angry at?

236
00:12:06,760 --> 00:12:08,560
Every crash wants a villain.

237
00:12:08,560 --> 00:12:10,160
2008 had the banks.

238
00:12:10,160 --> 00:12:12,360
Thursday doesn't really have one.

239
00:12:12,360 --> 00:12:14,480
I just didn't want the bill.

240
00:12:14,480 --> 00:12:16,760
I just wanted to keep my good year.

241
00:12:16,760 --> 00:12:18,600
I just said not yet.

242
00:12:18,600 --> 00:12:20,880
I just sold chips.

243
00:12:20,880 --> 00:12:23,540
Every one of those choices made sense on its own.

244
00:12:23,540 --> 00:12:26,000
They just all landed on the same morning.

245
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:28,400
So here's why the stock market crashed.

246
00:12:28,400 --> 00:12:32,600
A market that's one-third AI had been sliding for a month.

247
00:12:32,920 --> 00:12:35,600
In three days, voters elected a Congress

248
00:12:35,600 --> 00:12:37,640
that might reign in data centers.

249
00:12:37,640 --> 00:12:40,880
Beijing left 600,000 chips at the door.

250
00:12:40,880 --> 00:12:43,880
And funds sitting on a great year cashed out.

251
00:12:43,880 --> 00:12:44,880
Nothing broke.

252
00:12:44,880 --> 00:12:46,080
The fear did it.

253
00:12:46,080 --> 00:12:47,640
It isn't new either.

254
00:12:47,640 --> 00:12:51,360
In the 1840s, Britain went mad for railways.

255
00:12:51,360 --> 00:12:54,960
And at the peak, it was pouring about 7% of its economy

256
00:12:54,960 --> 00:12:55,840
into track.

257
00:12:55,840 --> 00:12:59,160
Railway shares lost nearly 2 thirds of their value.

258
00:12:59,160 --> 00:13:02,480
And a third or more of the approved lines never got built.

259
00:13:02,520 --> 00:13:04,640
But many of the lines that did get built

260
00:13:04,640 --> 00:13:07,000
are still part of Britain's railway today.

261
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:08,200
Here's the catch.

262
00:13:08,200 --> 00:13:10,640
A railway can wait for its trains.

263
00:13:10,640 --> 00:13:13,720
A chip sitting in a dark building just gets older.

264
00:13:13,720 --> 00:13:17,000
So was Thursday the AI bubble popping?

265
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:17,920
Not yet.

266
00:13:17,920 --> 00:13:21,760
Both tariff crash days in April, 2025 were bigger.

267
00:13:21,760 --> 00:13:26,200
And together erased a record $6.6 trillion.

268
00:13:26,200 --> 00:13:30,040
Jobless claims were near their lowest since 1969.

269
00:13:30,040 --> 00:13:32,000
And no rule had passed.

270
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:34,880
Bernie Sanders called for a national moratorium

271
00:13:34,880 --> 00:13:36,240
on data centers.

272
00:13:36,240 --> 00:13:38,800
Americans don't want big tech oligarchs

273
00:13:38,800 --> 00:13:40,320
wrecking the environment.

274
00:13:40,320 --> 00:13:42,160
A call, not a bill.

275
00:13:42,160 --> 00:13:44,760
A bubble doesn't pop when prices fall.

276
00:13:44,760 --> 00:13:46,880
It pops when the money stops.

277
00:13:46,880 --> 00:13:49,560
On Thursday, the money hadn't stopped.

278
00:13:49,560 --> 00:13:51,800
NVIDIA's sales were still doubling.

279
00:13:51,800 --> 00:13:54,120
The giants were still expected to spend more

280
00:13:54,120 --> 00:13:56,240
than a trillion dollars next year.

281
00:13:56,240 --> 00:13:59,040
But here's the part almost nobody noticed.

282
00:13:59,040 --> 00:14:02,320
A week before the crash, one giant blinked.

283
00:14:02,320 --> 00:14:04,160
Microsoft cut its spending plan

284
00:14:04,160 --> 00:14:08,240
from $175 billion to 170.

285
00:14:08,240 --> 00:14:10,840
That's less than 3%, tiny.

286
00:14:10,840 --> 00:14:13,360
But it's a foot moving toward the break.

287
00:14:13,360 --> 00:14:16,200
And on Tuesday, the people who pay the electric bill

288
00:14:16,200 --> 00:14:18,480
elected a Congress that might want to say

289
00:14:18,480 --> 00:14:19,920
in where the buildings go.

290
00:14:19,920 --> 00:14:21,880
So Thursday wasn't the pop.

291
00:14:21,880 --> 00:14:24,520
It was the day the market noticed there's a pin.

292
00:14:24,520 --> 00:14:27,520
And that voters, Washington and Beijing,

293
00:14:27,520 --> 00:14:28,960
all have a hand on it.

294
00:14:28,960 --> 00:14:32,600
NVIDIA is now about a quarter below its October record.

295
00:14:32,600 --> 00:14:35,680
Its next report is due on the 17th of November.

296
00:14:35,680 --> 00:14:39,080
And the 600,000 chips are still in Hong Kong.

297
00:14:39,080 --> 00:14:41,480
The rally died in six minutes.

298
00:14:41,480 --> 00:14:44,680
What happens next gets decided much more slowly

299
00:14:44,680 --> 00:14:47,840
by permits, power lines, and an electric bill

300
00:14:47,840 --> 00:14:49,800
on a kitchen table in Ohio.

301
00:14:49,800 --> 00:14:52,360
On Thursday, Lisa Su said the market

302
00:14:52,360 --> 00:14:54,680
can be a little bit short-sighted.

303
00:14:54,680 --> 00:14:57,080
That afternoon, it might have been the furthest

304
00:14:57,080 --> 00:14:59,400
it had looked in months.

